WHY GPL-3, AND NOT MIT
----------------------
This was not a stylistic choice. EasyAnalysis requires, at run time, R packages
that are licensed GPL-3 (or GPL-2|GPL-3) and offer no permissive alternative --
among them shinyWidgets, nnet, MASS, ape, lidR, terra, leaflet.extras,
base64enc and ggspatial. A combined work that cannot function without GPL-3
libraries has to be distributed under GPL-3-compatible terms, so GPL-3 is the
honest description of what a user actually receives.

Releasing the app itself as MIT would not have changed that: the moment it is
distributed and run alongside those packages, the effective terms of the whole
are GPL-3. Stating GPL-3 up front avoids implying a freedom that does not exist.

Note that many other dependencies (shiny, bslib, DT, ggplot2, leaflet, httr,
jsonlite, ...) are MIT or Apache. Those are GPL-compatible and impose no
additional restriction; they are simply included on their own terms.

Each dependency remains under its own licence. This notice covers the
EasyAnalysis source code only.
